  "query_preview": "../Monday through Thursday/...The low pressure system \nbringing widespread rainfall to the panhandle Sunday will \ncontinue to shift southeastward through Monday, moving out of the \nnorthern panhandle and lingering over the southern and parts of \nthe central panhandle through the day. The models have come more \nin line on the low positioning, indicating that the southern \npanhandle will see the highest rainfall amounts with this system. \nAs the low moves in from the west, the northern panhandle should \nsee the front move in first before rainfall quickly spreads to the\nrest of the panhandle through Sunday morning. Most of this rain \nwill fall overnight into early Monday morning, tapering off from \nnorthwest to southeast through Monday evening. Expecting around an\ninch and a half of rainfall for parts of the southern panhandle \nin 24 hours from early Monday morning through early Tuesday \nmorning. Light winds are expected for a majority of the panhandle \nbehind the front, though sea breezes are expected to increase \nthrough some of the inner channels Tuesday afternoon, particularly\nin Lynn Canal.\n\nPotential for a break continues to increase for the early week as\nthe low dips south, though keeping an eye on upper level easterly\nflow that may allow for showers in BC to traverse into the \npanhandle. The northern and central panhandle have potential to \nsee some sun behind this front on Monday, with the southern \npanhandle joining in by Tuesday. The slight period of ridging that\ndevelops over the eastern gulf should allow winds in the inner \nchannels to switch to a more offshore direction overnight before \nthe weather becomes more active through the second half of the \nweek. With the potential for clearing, sea breezes should increase\nthrough midday and turn winds back onshore for a period. Daytime \nhigh temperatures will also increase above normal for days that \nsee this clearing, allowing for highs reaching the high 60s to low\n70s across the panhandle Tuesday. The next system is expected to \nreach the panhandle through Wednesday. NBM PoP is getting washed \nout through the end of the week, so slightly increased confidence \nthrough the northern panhandle with the onset of the front.",
